think The receipt-key ruling is still Keaton's. Round ten wrote Option A and Option B for it, and a round that adds a tenth hold teaches nothing, so this round answers the free question round ten named instead: how many commits since the last green receipt would have moved a watched digest.
think The receipt in construction/standing-equipment-receipt.kyri records head fa310d296b. git merge-base --is-ancestor says that commit is NOT an ancestor of HEAD, so the basis names a commit this line does not contain. That is a finding about the basis itself, reported as seen, not explained.
think The count runs over commits reachable from HEAD and not from the receipt head. The watched set is not yet ruled. This reading assumes the watched set is everything outside logs, cards, active rooms, prompts, foundations, context, docs-geode, research, and .claude. Keaton's ruling may draw that set differently, so the split below is one assumption's answer.
think No code, witness, pin, or claim touched. The compile layer and the roster runner stay as they were, so the cold endurance run and the claim are not owed by this lap.
obs Under that assumed set, 9083 commits sit between the receipt head and HEAD: 4701 touch a path outside it and 4382 touch only logs, cards, or rooms the set leaves out. Roughly half the commits since the basis would not move a watched key under Option A with this set.
obs That share is the cost-side evidence for Option A and only that. It does not show the cold-run saving, because a skipped run needs the receipt to be current, and the receipt head is not in this line.
obs The reading is a commit-set count, not a tree-diff count. A commit that adds and removes the same watched file reads as touching it. Run the tree diff before quoting the saving as a number.
rota lap 9083, row 3 -- water tastes; the reading surfaced that a basis naming a commit outside this line is the flow gone off, and that the honest move is to name the off taste and leave the ruling where it sits.
